window.GUIDE={"slug":"realism","name":"Realism","img":"/study/art-history/realism/img/","themeLabel":"Theme","parts":[{"h":1,"title":"Barbizon and the Open Air","section":"Realism in France"},{"h":2,"title":"Courbet","section":"Realism in France"},{"h":3,"title":"Peasants and Labor","section":"Realism in France"},{"h":4,"title":"The City and the Street","section":"Realism in France"},{"h":5,"title":"Britain and Europe","section":"Realism Abroad"},{"h":6,"title":"America","section":"Realism Abroad"}],"items":[{"id":"start","url":"/study/art-history/realism.html","h":0,"kind":"intro","label":"About this site","title":"About this site","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"About this site"},{"id":"h1-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/barbizon-and-the-open-air.html","h":1,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Barbizon and the Open Air","artist":"","thumb":"corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","text":"Barbizon and the Open Air · Landscape painters around Fontainebleau and the Oise, 1830s–1860s · Beginning before Realism had a name, a group of landscape painters worked from close study in the forest near the village of Barbizon, south of Paris, and along the Oise to the north. They painted real French places, and their example opened the way for painting ordinary life. · Corot's forest and a remembered lake · Rousseau, Diaz and Daubigny: named places, real light · A State-commissioned farm scene · A photographer in the same woods · Camille Corot · Forest of Fontainebleau · 1834"},{"id":"corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/forest-of-fontainebleau.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Forest of Fontainebleau","title":"Forest of Fontainebleau","artist":"Camille Corot","thumb":"corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","text":"Forest of Fontainebleau · Camille Corot · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 175.6 × 242.6 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A large forest view, painted for the Salon of 1834 from sketches made outdoors. · It was still framed as a historic landscape: viewers took the woman reading in the foreground for Mary Magdalene. · The National Gallery notes the \"clarity and freshness of vision\" that set Corot apart from formula landscapes. · The forest, not the small figure, holds the eye. · Salon · the official annual or biennial art exhibition in Paris, where artists built their reputations. · Historic landscape · a landscape with a story from history, myth or the Bible, a category meant to raise landscape's status."},{"id":"corot-souvenir-of-mortefontaine","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/souvenir-of-mortefontaine.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Souvenir of Mortefontaine","title":"Souvenir of Mortefontaine","artist":"Camille Corot","thumb":"corot-souvenir-of-mortefontaine","text":"Souvenir of Mortefontaine · Camille Corot · about 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 89 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A silvery lake scene recalled rather than recorded; the title means a remembrance. · Napoleon III bought it at the Salon of 1864; it entered the Louvre in 1889. · Look at the feathery trees and soft gray-green light, Corot's late manner. · It shows the road Realism did not take: a dreamy, remembered landscape, the kind of official taste Realism pushed against."},{"id":"rousseau-sunset-fontainebleau","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/sunset-from-the-forest-of-fontainebleau.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau","title":"Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau","artist":"Théodore Rousseau","thumb":"rousseau-sunset-fontainebleau","text":"Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau · Théodore Rousseau · 1848 · Oil on paper over black chalk or conté crayon, laid down on canvas, 32.2 × 45.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A small study of the setting sun through the trees, with cattle near water. · Rousseau settled at Barbizon and was a leading figure of the group. · It is painted on paper over a chalk drawing, a small, quick format suited to recording light. · Its subject is one hour of light in one real place, not an ideal or a story. · Barbizon school · landscape painters, among them Rousseau, Diaz, Daubigny and Troyon, who worked in and around the Forest of Fontainebleau from the 1830s."},{"id":"diaz-edge-of-forest","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-edge-of-the-forest-at-les-monts-girard-fontainebleau.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau","title":"The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau","artist":"Narcisse Diaz de la Peña","thumb":"diaz-edge-of-forest","text":"The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau · Narcisse Diaz de la Peña · 1868 · Oil on canvas, 97.8 × 125.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Storm light breaks over dark trees at a named spot in the forest. · Look at the heavy, broken paint in the foliage. · Dark, dense paint gives the trees weight and the light its brightness by contrast."},{"id":"daubigny-washerwomen-oise","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/washerwomen-at-the-oise-river-near-valmondois.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois","title":"Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois","artist":"Charles-François Daubigny","thumb":"daubigny-washerwomen-oise","text":"Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois · Charles-François Daubigny · 1865 · Oil on wood, 24 × 46 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A wide, quiet river with two small figures doing laundry at the water's edge. · The panel is small, the size of a study, though it is signed and dated. · Valmondois is on the Oise, north of Paris, the river Daubigny painted again and again. · A low horizon gives most of the panel to sky and water."},{"id":"troyon-oxen-going-to-work","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/oxen-going-to-work-morning-effect.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect","title":"Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect","artist":"Constant Troyon","thumb":"troyon-oxen-going-to-work","text":"Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect · Constant Troyon · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 262 × 391 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris (on deposit from the Louvre) · Oxen head out to plow at sunrise, painted on a canvas nearly four meters wide. · The State commissioned it in 1854 and showed it at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Look at the low sun behind the team and the long shadows thrown toward the viewer. · A farm-animal subject on a canvas nearly four meters wide, at the scale of history painting. · History painting · large pictures of history, religion or myth, ranked highest by the academies."},{"id":"cuvelier-forest-scene","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/carrefour-de-lepine.html","h":1,"kind":"work","label":"Carrefour de l'Epine","title":"Carrefour de l'Epine","artist":"Eugène Cuvelier","thumb":"cuvelier-forest-scene","text":"Carrefour de l'Epine · Eugène Cuvelier · early 1860s · Salted paper print, 19.3 × 25.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A photograph of bare trees at the Carrefour de l'Épine, a named crossing in the Forest of Fontainebleau. · Cuvelier worked in the circle of Corot and photographed the same woods the painters used. · The Met's Cuvelier study notes that he printed from paper negatives, which give the soft, grainy tone. · The photograph shares the painters' aim: a real spot, recorded without arranging it. · Salted paper print · an early photographic print on paper coated with salt and silver, with a matte surface."},{"id":"h2-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/courbet.html","h":2,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Courbet","artist":"","thumb":"courbet-painters-studio","text":"Courbet · The painter who claimed the name Realist · Gustave Courbet, from the village of Ornans in eastern France, painted laborers, villagers and his own friends, often at the size of history painting. Critics attacked him, and he answered with his own exhibition and a manifesto. · The road workers and the village funeral · The great studio allegory · Village girls, a roadside meeting and two young women by the Seine · Landscape and sea from his own region and the coast · Gustave Courbet · The Painter's Studio · 1854–55"},{"id":"courbet-stonebreakers","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stonebreakers.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Stonebreakers","title":"The Stonebreakers","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-stonebreakers","text":"The Stonebreakers · Gustave Courbet · 1849 · Oil on canvas. Formerly Gemäldegalerie, Dresden; lost during the Second World War · Two road workers, one too old and one too young for the job, break stones by a roadside. · Shown at the Salon of 1850–51; the Dresden picture is known today from photographs, and a smaller version survives in Winterthur. · In a letter he said the two men he saw by the road were \"the most complete expression of poverty.\" · Their faces are turned away or shaded, so they stand for a kind of work rather than two individuals. · Jonathan Ribner of Boston University contrasts its stiff, piece-by-piece composition with the flowing unity of Millet's figures. · One of the first pictures to show manual labor plainly, without uplift."},{"id":"courbet-burial-at-ornans","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/a-burial-at-ornans.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"A Burial at Ornans","title":"A Burial at Ornans","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-burial-at-ornans","text":"A Burial at Ornans · Gustave Courbet · 1849–50 · Oil on canvas, 315 × 668 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A village funeral, with Courbet's family and neighbors as the mourners, on a canvas almost seven meters long. · Shown at the same Salon as The Stonebreakers; critics called it vulgar and ugly. · The Musée d'Orsay points to the visible brushwork and deep blacks, applied with brush and palette knife, against academic polish. · Who is being buried is unknown. · Ordinary villagers painted life-size, a scale normally given to religious and mythic subjects, made it the manifesto of the new style."},{"id":"courbet-painters-studio","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-painters-studio.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Painter's Studio","title":"The Painter's Studio","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-painters-studio","text":"The Painter's Studio · Gustave Courbet · 1854–55 · Oil on canvas, 361 × 598 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Courbet paints a landscape at the center of a crowded studio. · Its full title calls it a \"real allegory\" of seven years of his artistic life. · On the right are friends and backers, among them Baudelaire reading; a hunter on the left may have Napoleon III's features. · From 1899 to 1919 it hung as the backdrop of a private amateur theater in Paris. · He turns his own studio and career into the subject of a monumental picture."},{"id":"h2-4","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/theme-the-pavilion-of-realism.html","h":2,"kind":"theme","label":"The Pavilion of Realism","title":"The Pavilion of Realism","artist":"","thumb":"courbet-burial-at-ornans","text":"The Pavilion of Realism · In 1855, after the Exposition Universelle jury turned down The Painter's Studio, Courbet built his own pavilion and showed his work there. · He paid for it himself and put it within sight of the official exhibition. The show included The Painter's Studio and A Burial at Ornans, with more than forty works in all. · Its catalogue carried his Realist manifesto, promising subjects from modern life. · In the catalogue preface he wrote that the title of Realist had been imposed on him. · It was a step outside the official system that later independent shows, including the Impressionists' in 1874, took further."},{"id":"courbet-young-ladies-village","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-of-the-village.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Young Ladies of the Village","title":"Young Ladies of the Village","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-young-ladies-village","text":"Young Ladies of the Village · Gustave Courbet · 1851–52 · Oil on canvas, 194.9 × 261 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Courbet's three sisters, out walking near Ornans; one gives alms to a young cowherd. · Salon critics in 1852 found it crude and mocked the models' plain features and country dress. · X-rays show Courbet enlarged the figures as he worked. · Calling country girls \"demoiselles\", young ladies, blurred class lines, part of what unsettled Paris critics."},{"id":"courbet-bonjour-monsieur-courbet","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-meeting-bonjour-monsieur-courbet.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet)","title":"The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet)","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-bonjour-monsieur-courbet","text":"The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet) · Gustave Courbet · 1854 · Oil on canvas, 132 × 150 cm. Musée Fabre, Montpellier · Alfred Bruyas, a collector, and his servant greet Courbet on a country road; the painter has his gear strapped to his back. · Courbet himself reported the nickname \"Bonjour Monsieur Courbet\" in an 1855 letter. · Unlike The Painter's Studio, it was accepted for the 1855 Exposition Universelle, where caricaturists mocked its vanity. · Bruyas gave it to the Musée Fabre in 1868. · Painter and patron meet as equals on a dusty road, with the artist shown as a working traveler."},{"id":"courbet-young-ladies-seine","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-on-the-banks-of-the-seine-summer.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer)","title":"Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer)","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-young-ladies-seine","text":"Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer) · Gustave Courbet · 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 174 × 206 cm. Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris · Two young Parisian women lie on the riverbank on a summer day, one half asleep. · It caused a critical scandal at the Salon of 1857. · The Petit Palais finds its realism in \"the frankness of the faces and postures.\" · Rumpled clothes and unguarded poses, with no classical pretext, carry the picture."},{"id":"courbet-stream-puits-noir","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stream-le-ruisseau-du-puits-noir-vallee-de-la-loue.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue)","title":"The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue)","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-stream-puits-noir","text":"The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue) · Gustave Courbet · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 104 × 137 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A shaded stream in a rocky valley near Ornans, thought to be the first of several views of the site. · Shown at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Courbet laid on paint with a palette knife in irregular layers, the National Gallery notes. · Rough paint for rough rock: the handling itself feels like the place."},{"id":"courbet-the-wave","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-wave.html","h":2,"kind":"work","label":"The Wave","title":"The Wave","artist":"Gustave Courbet","thumb":"courbet-the-wave","text":"The Wave · Gustave Courbet · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 65.6 × 92.4 cm. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · A wave breaks under a stormy sky on the Normandy coast near Étretat. · The Städel says he painted the scene about sixty times between 1865 and 1870. · Look at the thick paint of the foam against the dark water. · Sea and weather alone, with no figures or story, are the whole subject."},{"id":"h3-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/peasants-and-labor.html","h":3,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Peasants and Labor","artist":"","thumb":"millet-gleaners","text":"Peasants and Labor · Millet, Bonheur and the painters of rural France, 1849–1884 · After the Revolution of 1848, peasants and farm work became a major subject. Some painters gave farm labor dignity and grandeur; others showed its toll, and were accused of politics or ugliness. · Millet's sower, gleaners, angelus, hoe and rake · Rosa Bonheur's working animals · Breton's and Bastien-Lepage's later naturalism · Jean-François Millet · The Gleaners · 1857"},{"id":"millet-sower","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-sower.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Sower","title":"The Sower","artist":"Jean-François Millet","thumb":"millet-sower","text":"The Sower · Jean-François Millet · 1850 · Oil on canvas, 101.6 × 82.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · At dusk a peasant flings seed as he strides downhill, oxen harrowing the field behind him. · Ribner sees a near-biblical dignity in the sower that the stonebreakers lack. · The MFA quotes a 19th-century critic: he \"seems painted with the earth that he sows.\" · The American painter William Morris Hunt bought it from Millet around 1851. · One field worker given the scale and stride of a hero, painted soon after the 1848 Revolution gave male farm laborers the vote."},{"id":"millet-gleaners","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-gleaners.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Gleaners","title":"The Gleaners","artist":"Jean-François Millet","thumb":"millet-gleaners","text":"The Gleaners · Jean-François Millet · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 83.5 × 110 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Three women bend to pick up stray grain left after the harvest, while the main harvest goes on far behind them. · Shown at the Salon of 1857, after nearly a decade spent on the subject. · A rider at the right, probably an estate overseer, marks who is in charge. · Ribner notes that the women never look capable of revolt, though some viewers read the picture as subversive. · Three bent figures given the weight of sculpture, not the pathos of charity. · Gleaning · gathering what the harvesters leave behind, a right traditionally allowed to the rural poor."},{"id":"millet-angelus","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-angelus.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Angelus","title":"The Angelus","artist":"Jean-François Millet","thumb":"millet-angelus","text":"The Angelus · Jean-François Millet · 1857–59 · Oil on canvas, 55.5 × 66 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A man and woman stop digging potatoes to pray as the evening bell rings. · Millet said the idea came from his grandmother, who stopped work to pray whenever the bell rang. · It became so famous that its sale abroad in 1889 caused an outcry in France; a man slashed it in 1932. · Prayer appears as part of the working day, in a real field with real tools. · Angelus · a Catholic prayer said at morning, noon and evening when a church bell rings."},{"id":"millet-man-with-a-hoe","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/man-with-a-hoe.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Man with a Hoe","title":"Man with a Hoe","artist":"Jean-François Millet","thumb":"millet-man-with-a-hoe","text":"Man with a Hoe · Jean-François Millet · 1860–62 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 100.3 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · An exhausted laborer leans on his hoe in a stony field full of thistles. · At the Salon of 1863 it caused a storm; Parisian viewers found the man brutish and frightening. · It was read as socialist protest; Millet denied being a socialist or a political agitator. · Millet wrote that he tried to say \"what I saw and felt\" at work in the fields. · No ennobling here: the cost of labor is written on the body."},{"id":"millet-woman-with-rake","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/woman-with-a-rake.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Woman with a Rake","title":"Woman with a Rake","artist":"Jean-François Millet","thumb":"millet-woman-with-rake","text":"Woman with a Rake · Jean-François Millet · probably 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 39.7 × 34.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A single field worker rakes hay beside a haystack, the harvest going on behind her. · Millet first used the subject in a woodcut for a popular series on farm work, published in 1853. · It was shown in 1860 at the Martinet gallery, where Courbet and Manet also exhibited. · A subject first made for cheap prints became an easel painting."},{"id":"bonheur-ploughing-nivernais","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/ploughing-in-the-nivernais.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Ploughing in the Nivernais","title":"Ploughing in the Nivernais","artist":"Rosa Bonheur","thumb":"bonheur-ploughing-nivernais","text":"Ploughing in the Nivernais · Rosa Bonheur · 1849 · Oil on canvas, 133 × 260 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two teams of oxen make the first ploughing of fallow land in central France, known as sombrage. · The State commissioned it in 1848; shown at the Salon of 1849, it was praised almost unanimously. · The Musée d'Orsay calls it a hymn to work in the fields. · The animals are studied as closely as portraits, and the land is a real region."},{"id":"bonheur-horse-fair","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-horse-fair.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Horse Fair","title":"The Horse Fair","artist":"Rosa Bonheur","thumb":"bonheur-horse-fair","text":"The Horse Fair · Rosa Bonheur · 1852–55 · Oil on canvas, 244.5 × 506.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Grooms lead and ride horses at the Paris horse market on the boulevard de l'Hôpital. · Shown at the Salon of 1853, where it was widely praised; she retouched it in 1855. · The dome of the Salpêtrière shows in the left background. · A working Paris market scene, painted about five meters wide."},{"id":"breton-song-of-the-lark","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-song-of-the-lark.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"The Song of the Lark","title":"The Song of the Lark","artist":"Jules Breton","thumb":"breton-song-of-the-lark","text":"The Song of the Lark · Jules Breton · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 110.6 × 85.8 cm. Art Institute of Chicago · A young peasant woman with a sickle stops at sunrise to listen to a lark. · First shown at the Salon of 1885. · In 1934 it was named \"America's Best Loved Picture\" in a Chicago newspaper contest. · Breton was raised in Courrières, northern France, and painted its fields for much of his career. · A softer, more idealized vision of rural life than Millet's or Courbet's."},{"id":"bastien-lepage-haymaking","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/haymaking.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Haymaking","title":"Haymaking","artist":"Jules Bastien-Lepage","thumb":"bastien-lepage-haymaking","text":"Haymaking · Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 195 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two hay-makers rest in a mown field near his home village of Damvillers. · A great success at the Salon of 1878; the State bought it from his heirs in 1885. · Émile Zola called him \"grandson of Millet and Courbet\" and the picture the masterpiece of naturalism in painting. · The horizon sits near the top and the figures crowd the front edge, a cropping close to photography. · A later generation's naturalism: the workers' exhaustion shown plainly. · Naturalism · a later, more detailed form of Realism; the name Zola used for art that recorded life as observed."},{"id":"bastien-lepage-joan-of-arc","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/joan-of-arc.html","h":3,"kind":"work","label":"Joan of Arc","title":"Joan of Arc","artist":"Jules Bastien-Lepage","thumb":"bastien-lepage-joan-of-arc","text":"Joan of Arc · Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1879 · Oil on canvas, 254 × 279.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A peasant girl in a farm garden in Lorraine starts up as the saints appear to her. · Shown at the Salon of 1880. · After France lost part of Lorraine to Germany in 1871, Joan became a patriotic symbol. · Critics praised her expression but thought the ghostly saints clashed with the realism of the rest. · A history subject painted as if observed on an ordinary farm."},{"id":"h4-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-city-and-the-street.html","h":4,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"The City and the Street","artist":"","thumb":"daumier-third-class-carriage","text":"The City and the Street · Daumier, early Manet and the changing face of Paris · In Paris, Realism meant the people of the street and the politics of the day. Daumier drew both for the press; Manet painted the city's poor and passers-by; a photographer recorded streets about to be torn down. · Daumier's political prints · Daumier's paintings of working Paris · Manet's street people, singer and dead bullfighter · Photographing old Paris · Honoré Daumier · The Third-Class Carriage · 1864"},{"id":"daumier-ventre-legislatif","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/le-ventre-legislatif-the-legislative-belly.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly)","title":"Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly)","artist":"Honoré Daumier","thumb":"daumier-ventre-legislatif","text":"Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly) · Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.2 × 43.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The government ministers and deputies of 1834, sitting in tiers, each drawn as a recognizable caricature. · Daumier had modeled many of these politicians as small painted clay busts, which curator John Daniel Tilford of the Oglethorpe University Museum of Art describes as models for his lithographs. · The French title puns on the legislative body: ventre means belly. · Satire in the press made current politics a subject for art. · Lithograph · a print drawn in greasy crayon on stone, so it could be printed quickly in large editions for the illustrated press and print series."},{"id":"daumier-rue-transnonain","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-transnonain-april-15-1834.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834","title":"Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834","artist":"Honoré Daumier","thumb":"daumier-rue-transnonain","text":"Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834 · Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.6 × 44.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man lies dead beside his bed, a child crushed under him, in an apartment after soldiers stormed the building and killed people inside. · It records a real event of the April 1834 riots in Paris. · After it appeared in a print-seller's window, Smarthistory reports, police tried to track down copies and confiscated the stone. · No hero and no symbol: one room, one family, as a witness might have found it."},{"id":"daumier-third-class-carriage","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-third-class-carriage.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Third-Class Carriage","title":"The Third-Class Carriage","artist":"Honoré Daumier","thumb":"daumier-third-class-carriage","text":"The Third-Class Carriage · Honoré Daumier · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65.4 × 90.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A nursing mother, an old woman with a basket and a sleeping boy ride on a crowded wooden bench. · The Met says it takes up a lithograph from about ten years earlier. · It is unfinished; look for the grid of squaring lines used to transfer the design. · Daumier showed how ordinary people lived with the new railways and the industrial city."},{"id":"daumier-laundress","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-laundress.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Laundress","title":"The Laundress","artist":"Honoré Daumier","thumb":"daumier-laundress","text":"The Laundress · Honoré Daumier · about 1863 · Oil on wood, 48.9 × 33 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A washerwoman with a heavy bundle helps a child up the steps from the Seine. · Daumier could see laundresses returning from the river's laundry boats from his studio on the quai d'Anjou. · The paint has decayed, so the final digit of the date can no longer be read. · The figure is a dark mass against a bright city wall, a type of the urban laborer rather than a portrait."},{"id":"manet-old-musician","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-old-musician.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Old Musician","title":"The Old Musician","artist":"Édouard Manet","thumb":"manet-old-musician","text":"The Old Musician · Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 187.4 × 248.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Five ragged figures gather around a bearded street violinist on waste ground. · First shown at the Galerie Martinet in Paris in 1863. · His models came from a poor neighborhood at the city's edge, just as Paris was being rebuilt around new boulevards. · Street people painted at a scale the National Gallery says was normally kept for the wealthy and powerful."},{"id":"manet-dead-toreador","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-dead-toreador.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"The Dead Toreador","title":"The Dead Toreador","artist":"Édouard Manet","thumb":"manet-dead-toreador","text":"The Dead Toreador · Édouard Manet · probably 1864 · Oil on canvas, 75.9 × 153.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A bullfighter lies dead on the ground, painted alone against a plain dark background. · It began as part of a larger picture shown at the Salon of 1864. · After critics and cartoonists mocked that canvas, Manet cut it up; the other piece, The Bullfight, is in the Frick Collection. · A contemporary death treated like a fact, not a drama."},{"id":"manet-street-singer","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/street-singer.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Street Singer","title":"Street Singer","artist":"Édouard Manet","thumb":"manet-street-singer","text":"Street Singer · Édouard Manet · about 1862 · Oil on canvas, 171.1 × 105.8 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A singer with a guitar steps out of a café, eating cherries from a paper parcel. · Manet saw a singer leave a café and later had Victorine Meurent pose for the figure in his studio. · The MFA quotes Zola praising Manet's ability to \"state frankly what he sees.\" · A chance moment of city life, with an awkward, unguarded gesture."},{"id":"marville-rue-saint-jacques","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-saint-jacques.html","h":4,"kind":"work","label":"Rue Saint-Jacques","title":"Rue Saint-Jacques","artist":"Charles Marville","thumb":"marville-rue-saint-jacques","text":"Rue Saint-Jacques · Charles Marville · 1864–67 · Albumen silver print, 30.6 × 27 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A narrow street in the Latin Quarter, photographed from a corner, looking down its length. · From 1862 Marville was the city of Paris's official photographer. · He photographed old streets slated for demolition in Baron Haussmann's rebuilding of the city. · Peeling posters and painted shop signs on the walls: the old street itself is the subject. · Albumen print · a photographic print made with egg white to hold the image, giving fine detail; the usual process from the 1850s to the 1890s."},{"id":"h5-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/britain-and-europe.html","h":5,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"Britain and Europe","artist":"","thumb":"repin-barge-haulers","text":"Britain and Europe · Realism in Britain, Germany and Russia · Painters across Europe took up modern life and working people, each in their own way: moral storytelling in Britain, close detail in Germany, social witness in Russia. · Britain: work, emigration, poverty and the railway · Germany: the factory, the village church, a mocked debut · Russia: the haulers and the returning exile · Ilya Repin · Barge Haulers on the Volga · 1870–73"},{"id":"brown-work","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/work.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Work","title":"Work","artist":"Ford Madox Brown","thumb":"brown-work","text":"Work · Ford Madox Brown · 1852–65 · Oil on canvas, 137 × 197.3 cm. Manchester Art Gallery · Navvies dig up a Hampstead street while people of every class pass by. · Brown showed it at his own exhibition in 1865, with a catalogue essay explaining the figures. · The two men at the right are portraits of the writers Thomas Carlyle and F. D. Maurice. · A street scene packed with real types becomes an argument about the value of labor. · Navvy · a laborer digging roads, canals or railways."},{"id":"brown-last-of-england","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-last-of-england.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Last of England","title":"The Last of England","artist":"Ford Madox Brown","thumb":"brown-last-of-england","text":"The Last of England · Ford Madox Brown · 1852–55 · Oil on panel, 82.5 × 75 cm. Birmingham Museums Trust · A young couple stare grimly ahead as their ship leaves England, the woman holding a baby's hand under her shawl. · The circular picture sits inside a painted gold border carrying its title. · Emigration, a real choice facing many Victorians, painted as a modern drama."},{"id":"fildes-casual-ward","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/applicants-for-admission-to-a-casual-ward.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward","title":"Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward","artist":"Luke Fildes","thumb":"fildes-casual-ward","text":"Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward · Luke Fildes · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 137.1 × 243.7 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Homeless men, women and children wait in the snow outside a London night shelter. · It grew from Fildes's 1869 illustration Houseless and Hungry for the weekly The Graphic. · At the Royal Academy in 1874, Royal Holloway says, the painting needed \"a barrier and a police officer\" to protect it from the crowds. · It was bought for Thomas Holloway in 1883. · The homeless poor, unidealized, given the finish of an Academy picture."},{"id":"frith-railway-station","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-railway-station.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Railway Station","title":"The Railway Station","artist":"William Powell Frith","thumb":"frith-railway-station","text":"The Railway Station · William Powell Frith · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 116.7 × 256.4 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Crowds board a train at Paddington Station: families, a wedding party, porters and passengers of every kind. · The dealer Louis Flatow bought the painting together with the rights to exhibit and reproduce it; more than 21,000 people came to see it in seven weeks. · At the right, two real, well-known London detectives arrest a man about to board. · Modern public life in all its variety, sold as a popular spectacle."},{"id":"menzel-iron-rolling-mill","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-iron-rolling-mill-modern-cyclopes.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes)","title":"The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes)","artist":"Adolph Menzel","thumb":"menzel-iron-rolling-mill","text":"The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes) · Adolph Menzel · 1872–75 · Oil on canvas, 158 × 254 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Workers drag a white-hot iron bloom through the rollers to make a railway rail. · In 1872 Menzel traveled to an ironworks in Upper Silesia to draw the machines and the men. · The Berlin museum notes that no factory owner commissioned it. · The museum calls a factory scene on this scale unprecedented in Europe."},{"id":"leibl-three-women-in-church","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/three-women-in-church.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Three Women in Church","title":"Three Women in Church","artist":"Wilhelm Leibl","thumb":"leibl-three-women-in-church","text":"Three Women in Church · Wilhelm Leibl · 1878–82 · Oil on mahogany panel, 113 × 77 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle · A young woman and two older ones sit in a pew in a Bavarian village church, one praying, two reading their prayer books. · Leibl worked on it for some three and a half years inside the church, very close to his models. · Look at the detail: embroidered clothes, rough hands, dust on the carved pew. · Real country women in an upright, altar-like format that honors Holbein; the museum calls it a main work of Realism."},{"id":"liebermann-women-plucking-geese","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/women-plucking-geese.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Women Plucking Geese","title":"Women Plucking Geese","artist":"Max Liebermann","thumb":"liebermann-women-plucking-geese","text":"Women Plucking Geese · Max Liebermann · 1871–72 · Oil on canvas, 118 × 172 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Women sit in a dim shed stripping feathers from geese while an old man brings more. · It was the first picture he showed in public, at about twenty-five, and it was mocked for its shabby subject and dark colors. · The sale paid for a journey to Paris, where he saw work by Millet and Courbet for the first time. · He took the broad, dark handling from the Hungarian painter Mihály Munkácsy, whose studio he had visited in 1871."},{"id":"repin-barge-haulers","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/barge-haulers-on-the-volga.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"Barge Haulers on the Volga","title":"Barge Haulers on the Volga","artist":"Ilya Repin","thumb":"repin-barge-haulers","text":"Barge Haulers on the Volga · Ilya Repin · 1870–73 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 281 cm. State Russian Museum, St Petersburg · Eleven men in harness drag a barge along the riverbank under a hot sky. · Repin first saw haulers on the Neva in 1868 and made two trips to the Volga to study them. · Unlike Courbet's faceless stonebreakers, each hauler is based on a portrait of a real man. · Shown at the Vienna World's Fair in 1873; the Russian Museum says it opened a new era in genre painting. · Labor shown as a long, grinding line of exhaustion across the whole width of the canvas."},{"id":"repin-did-not-expect-him","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/they-did-not-expect-him.html","h":5,"kind":"work","label":"They Did Not Expect Him","title":"They Did Not Expect Him","artist":"Ilya Repin","thumb":"repin-did-not-expect-him","text":"They Did Not Expect Him · Ilya Repin · 1884–88 · Oil on canvas, 160.5 × 167.5 cm. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · A political exile walks into his family's sitting room; the maid and the family freeze. · First shown in 1884 with the Wanderers. · Repin kept reworking the exile's face until 1888. · Family and friends posed, which keeps the scene grounded in a real home. · A political subject told through one ordinary moment of surprise. · The Wanderers (Peredvizhniki) · Russian Realist painters who formed in 1870 independent of the Academy, touring their exhibitions from 1871."},{"id":"h6-0","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/america.html","h":6,"kind":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"America","artist":"","thumb":"eakins-gross-clinic","text":"America · Homer, Eakins and Tanner after the Civil War · American painters applied Realism to the decades after the Civil War: soldiers, schoolboys, a rower, a surgeon at work, a music lesson, and the sea. · Homer's war and its aftermath · Childhood and the sea · Eakins's rowers and surgeons · Tanner's banjo lesson · Thomas Eakins · Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic) · 1875"},{"id":"homer-prisoners-from-the-front","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/prisoners-from-the-front.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Prisoners from the Front","title":"Prisoners from the Front","artist":"Winslow Homer","thumb":"homer-prisoners-from-the-front","text":"Prisoners from the Front · Winslow Homer · 1866 · Oil on canvas, 61 × 96.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Union officer faces three captured Confederate soldiers on a stripped battlefield. · The Met says it was inspired by the courage of Barlow, a Union general and friend of Homer's. · The critic Eugene Benson saw the prisoners as types standing for \"the elements in our Southern society that fomented and fed the rebellion.\" · The war is shown through faces and clothes rather than battle."},{"id":"homer-veteran-new-field","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-veteran-in-a-new-field.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Veteran in a New Field","title":"The Veteran in a New Field","artist":"Winslow Homer","thumb":"homer-veteran-new-field","text":"The Veteran in a New Field · Winslow Homer · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 61.3 × 96.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man with his back to us scythes ripe wheat; his Union jacket and canteen lie in the corner. · Finished in the months after Lee's surrender and Lincoln's assassination. · The Met notes the single-bladed scythe also recalls the Grim Reaper. · A soldier's return to farm work, shown plainly, which the Met reads as both grief and hope."},{"id":"homer-snap-the-whip","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/snap-the-whip.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Snap the Whip","title":"Snap the Whip","artist":"Winslow Homer","thumb":"homer-snap-the-whip","text":"Snap the Whip · Winslow Homer · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 30.5 × 50.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Barefoot boys play a rough chain game outside a red country schoolhouse. · This is the smaller of two versions; the larger is at the Butler Institute of American Art. · The Met reads the linked boys as a hopeful image of the reunited nation. · A schoolyard game, observed, becomes a picture of national memory as America grew more urban."},{"id":"homer-gulf-stream","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-gulf-stream.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Gulf Stream","title":"The Gulf Stream","artist":"Winslow Homer","thumb":"homer-gulf-stream","text":"The Gulf Stream · Winslow Homer · 1899, reworked by 1906 · Oil on canvas, 71.4 × 124.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Black sailor lies on the deck of a dismasted boat, sharks circling, a waterspout behind. · The Met notes that the current links the sugarcane on board to the history of transatlantic slavery. · Homer gives no outcome: whether the sailor is rescued is left open."},{"id":"eakins-champion-single-sculls","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-champion-single-sculls-max-schmitt-in-a-single-scull.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","title":"The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","artist":"Thomas Eakins","thumb":"eakins-champion-single-sculls","text":"The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull) · Thomas Eakins · 1871 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 117.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · His friend Max Schmitt rests his oars on the Schuylkill River in Philadelphia, in clear, raking light. · It is believed to mark Schmitt's racing victory of October 1870. · Eakins put himself in the picture, rowing farther back. · The Met calls it the work that launched his career. · Modern sport and a real place, worked out with careful perspective and light."},{"id":"eakins-gross-clinic","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/portrait-of-dr-samuel-d-gross-the-gross-clinic.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic)","title":"Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic)","artist":"Thomas Eakins","thumb":"eakins-gross-clinic","text":"Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic) · Thomas Eakins · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 243.8 × 199.4 cm. Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts · The surgeon Samuel Gross pauses, scalpel in his bloody hand, to lecture during an operation. · Made for the 1876 Centennial Exhibition; the fair's art jury turned it down, so it hung in a model army hospital. · Many viewers, the Philadelphia Museum of Art says, were repelled by its \"ugly and inartistic realism.\" · In a 2009 lecture, Kathleen Foster related its dark ground and dramatic light to old masters such as Rembrandt and Velázquez. · A modern operation, painted from close knowledge of the clinic, with nothing softened."},{"id":"tanner-banjo-lesson","url":"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-banjo-lesson.html","h":6,"kind":"work","label":"The Banjo Lesson","title":"The Banjo Lesson","artist":"Henry Ossawa Tanner","thumb":"tanner-banjo-lesson","text":"The Banjo Lesson · Henry Ossawa Tanner · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 124.5 × 90.2 cm. Hampton University Museum, Hampton, Virginia · An older man helps a boy hold a banjo, cool light from one side and warm firelight from the other. · It was admitted to the Paris Salon of 1894. · The same year Robert C. Ogden, chair of Hampton's board, bought it and gave it to Hampton. · In the same 2009 University of Pennsylvania lecture, Tanner was introduced as Eakins's \"great student.\" · An intimate lesson between generations, observed closely, offered a respectful picture of Black life."},{"id":"sources","url":"/study/art-history/realism/sources.html","h":99,"kind":"sources","label":"Sources and further reading","title":"Sources and further reading","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Sources and further reading · The text of this section was compiled for general readers from the sources below. The ideas and facts come from them; the wording is new, and any quotations are credited where they appear. · Foster, Kathleen A., and David B. Brownlee. “Thomas Eakins.” Penn Reading Project, University of Pennsylvania, 2009. YouTube video, 1:14:48. · Herdrich, Stephanie, and Sylvia Yount. “Winslow Homer: Crosscurrents, Virtual Opening.” The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2022. YouTube video, 24:47. · Ribner, Jonathan. “A New Look at Impressionism: Millet and the Painters of Barbizon.” Lecture, Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, 2018. YouTube video, 1:24:44. · Tilford, John Daniel. “The Arts and Satire of 19th Century France.” Oglethorpe University Museum of Art, 2015. YouTube video, 55:38. · Gersh-Nesic, Beth S. “Realism in Art.” Course syllabus, Purchase College, SUNY, spring 2014. · National Gallery of Art. Picturing France, 1830–1900. Teaching packet. Washington: National Gallery of Art. · Taube, Isabel. “Realism.” Course description, Department of Art History, Rutgers University. · Western Cape Education Department. “Visual Arts Grade 11, Term 1: Realism.” ePortal. · Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin. The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes), Adolph Menzel, A I 201. · Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin. Women Plucking Geese, Max Liebermann, A I 524. · Art Institute of Chicago. The Song of the Lark, Jules Breton, 1894.1033. · Birmingham Museums Trust. The Last of England, Ford Madox Brown, 1891P24. · Hamburger Kunsthalle. Three Women in Church, Wilhelm Leibl, HK-1534. · J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles. Man with a Hoe, Jean-François Millet, 85.PA.114. · Manchester Art Gallery. Work, Ford Madox Brown, 1885.1. · Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. Street Singer, Édouard Manet, 66.304. · Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. The Sower, Jean-François Millet, 17.1485. · Musée Fabre, Montpellier. The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet), Gustave Courbet, 868.1.23. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris (on deposit from the Louvre). Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect, Constant Troyon, RF 127. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. A Burial at Ornans, Gustave Courbet, RF 325. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. Haymaking, Jules Bastien-Lepage, RF 2748. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. Ploughing in the Nivernais, Rosa Bonheur, RF 64. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. The Angelus, Jean-François Millet, RF 1877. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. The Gleaners, Jean-François Millet, RF 592. · Musée d'Orsay, Paris. The Painter's Studio, Gustave Courbet, RF 2257. · Musée du Louvre, Paris. Souvenir of Mortefontaine, Camille Corot, MI 692 bis. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. Carrefour de l'Epine, Eugène Cuvelier, 1995.36.71. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. Forest of Fontainebleau, Camille Corot, 1963.10.109. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. Rue Saint-Jacques, Charles Marville, 2003.17.1. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau, Théodore Rousseau, 2003.70.1. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. The Dead Toreador, Édouard Manet, 1942.9.40. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau, Narcisse Diaz de la Peña, 2000.37.1. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. The Old Musician, Édouard Manet, 1963.10.162. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue), Gustave Courbet, 1943.15.2. · National Gallery of Art, Washington. Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois, Charles-François Daubigny, 1949.1.3. · Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris. Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer), Gustave Courbet. · Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts. Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic), Thomas Eakins, PAFA 2007.2; PMA 2007-1-1. · Royal Holloway, University of London. Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward, Luke Fildes, THC0021. · Royal Holloway, University of London. The Railway Station, William Powell Frith, THC0022. · State Russian Museum, St Petersburg. Barge Haulers on the Volga, Ilya Repin, Ж-4056. · Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main. The Wave, Gustave Courbet, 1433. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Joan of Arc, Jules Bastien-Lepage, 89.21.1. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly), Honoré Daumier, 20.60.5. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Prisoners from the Front, Winslow Homer, 22.207. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834, Honoré Daumier, 20.23. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Snap the Whip, Winslow Homer, 50.41. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull), Thomas Eakins, 34.92. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Gulf Stream, Winslow Homer, 06.1234. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Horse Fair, Rosa Bonheur, 87.25. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Laundress, Honoré Daumier, 47.122. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Third-Class Carriage, Honoré Daumier, 29.100.129. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. The Veteran in a New Field, Winslow Homer, 67.187.131. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Woman with a Rake, Jean-François Millet, 38.75. · The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. Young Ladies of the Village, Gustave Courbet, 40.175. · Finocchio, Ross. “Nineteenth-Century French Realism.” In Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History. New York: The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2004. · Galitz, Kathryn Calley. “Gustave Courbet (1819–1877).” In Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History. New York: The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2009. · Smarthistory. “Realism and Reality.” · Smarthistory. Daumier, Rue Transnonain. · Government Art Collection (UK). Entry on Francis Holl's engraving after Frith, The Railway Station. · Musée Fabre, Montpellier. La rencontre (education sheet, PDF). · Hampton University Museum. Page on Henry Ossawa Tanner, The Banjo Lesson."},{"id":"credits","url":"/study/art-history/realism/credits.html","h":99,"kind":"credits","label":"Image credits","title":"Image credits","artist":"","thumb":null,"text":"Image credits"}]};
